Android 15 / API 35: register the application-restrictions hook under the Binder
method name the platform actually invokes.

SYMPTOM
    A cloned app crashes when it asks for its own managed-configuration restrictions.
    Chrome does this during startup on a CrAsyncTask worker and dies:

        java.lang.SecurityException: Only system may: get application restrictions for
            other user/app com.android.chrome
          at android.os.IUserManager$Stub$Proxy.getApplicationRestrictionsForUser
          at android.os.UserManager.getApplicationRestrictions

ANDROID 15 / API 35 BINDER METHOD NAME
    UserManager.getApplicationRestrictions(String packageName)      <- public SDK
        invokes
    IUserManager.getApplicationRestrictionsForUser(String packageName, int userId)
                                                                    <- @hide Binder method

    The SDK name and the Binder name differ. Reading the SDK signature alone hides this;
    the stack trace above is where it is visible.

OLD BCORE REGISTRATION
    @ProxyMethod("getApplicationRestrictions")
    public static class GetApplicationRestrictions extends MethodHook { ... }

NEW REGISTRATION
    @ProxyMethods({"getApplicationRestrictions", "getApplicationRestrictionsForUser"})
    public static class GetApplicationRestrictions extends MethodHook { ... }

WHY THE OLD REGISTRATION NEVER FIRES ON API 35
    ClassInvocationStub#initAnnotation registers a hook by *name*:

        mMethodHookMap.put(name, methodHook);

    and it reads both @ProxyMethod (one name) and @ProxyMethods (several). The invoked
    Binder name on API 35 is getApplicationRestrictionsForUser, which was not in the map,
    so the call was never intercepted and went to the real IUserManager unchanged. The
    platform then refused it, because the Binder calling UID is the host app's while the
    package argument was the guest's -- two identities that do not correspond.

    @ProxyMethods is not new API for this fix: IAccessibilityManagerProxy$ReplaceUserId
    already registers seven names this way. This patch uses the existing idiom.

WHY THE HOOK BODY IS UNCHANGED
    The body was already correct:

        args[0] = BlackBoxCore.getHostPkg();
        return method.invoke(who, args);

    args[0] is the packageName in BOTH signatures -- getApplicationRestrictions(pkg) and
    getApplicationRestrictionsForUser(pkg, userId) -- so rewriting index 0 is right for
    each. Only the registration was wrong. userId (index 1 on the long form) is left
    untouched, so no other Android user is ever addressed.

SECURITY RATIONALE
    This is not a bypass, and the distinction is the point of the patch:

    - No UID, package, signature or certificate is spoofed. The guest app is not made to
      look like a system app and no SecurityException is suppressed.
    - The platform rule is "the caller must own the package it asks about". Inside a
      container the caller genuinely IS the host app, so asking about the host package is
      the truthful request, not a forged one. The platform check still runs and still
      passes on its own terms.
    - A virtual user is not an Android user, so a guest has no separate restrictions object
      at the platform level. The container owner's restrictions are the correct and only
      legitimate answer.
    - userId is not modified, so this cannot reach another real Android user's data.
    - A guest cannot use this to read a third-party app's restrictions: the package
      argument is replaced with the host package, never with an arbitrary one.
    - Play Integrity, SafetyNet, GMS and account/OAuth paths are untouched.

    On a device with no managed profile the host itself returns an empty Bundle, so the
    practical effect is that a clone receives the same empty Bundle the host receives,
    instead of crashing.

VALIDATION METHOD
    compatibility_test_ladder/usermanagerprobe -- a diagnostic with no dependencies at all,
    so the identical APK runs on the host and inside a container and any difference is
    attributable to the virtualization layer. It calls
    getApplicationRestrictions(getPackageName()) -- always its OWN package -- and records
    only success/failure, the returned Bundle's key count and the exception. It never
    queries another app, never another Android user, and never records a restriction value.

    Two controls run in the same process: getUserRestrictions() and isSystemUser(), which
    must keep succeeding. They show UserManager is reachable in the container, so a failure
    of the package-scoped call is specific rather than a dead service.

    Before this patch:  host PASS, guest SecurityException naming the guest package.
    Expected after:     host PASS, guest PASS with an empty Bundle, controls still PASS.

